CAT = "aesiraFirstAid"; SEG = "AID"
Q = [
("A guard reaches a person lying still on a tower forecourt. What is the first thing to check?",
 "Danger to you or others", ["Their pockets for ID", "Their pulse at the wrist", "The nearest CCTV view"],
 "A rescuer who becomes a casualty cannot help. Check for traffic, electricity or other danger, then check for a response."),
("An adult is unresponsive and not breathing normally. After calling 998, what should the guard start?",
 "Chest compressions", ["Back blows", "The recovery position", "Giving sips of water"],
 "Unresponsive with no normal breathing means cardiac arrest is likely. Start CPR with compressions and send for an AED."),
("For adult CPR, how should chest compressions be given?",
 "Centre of chest, 5 to 6 cm deep", ["Over the stomach, 2 cm deep", "Left side of chest, very slowly", "On the lower ribs, gently"],
 "Push hard on the centre of the chest, at least 5 cm but not more than 6 cm, letting it rise fully between compressions."),
("A guard doing CPR counts about one compression every two seconds. What should change?",
 "Push faster, near two a second", ["Push slower to save energy", "Nothing, the rate is right", "Stop to check the pulse often"],
 "Guidelines advise 100 to 120 compressions a minute, about two a second. One every two seconds is only 30 a minute, far too slow to move blood."),
("A trained rescuer giving adult CPR with rescue breaths uses which ratio?",
 "30 compressions to 2 breaths", ["15 compressions to 2 breaths", "5 compressions to 1 breath", "30 compressions to 5 breaths"],
 "The adult ratio is 30:2. If the rescuer cannot or will not give breaths, continuous compressions are still worthwhile."),
("When an AED arrives during CPR, what should the guard do?",
 "Use it as it instructs", ["Wait for the ambulance first", "Read the full manual first", "Use it only on children"],
 "AEDs talk the user through each step. Using one early greatly improves survival from cardiac arrest."),
("Why is an unresponsive person who is breathing normally put in the recovery position?",
 "To keep the airway open", ["To stop all bleeding", "To make CPR easier later", "To keep them warm and dry"],
 "Lying on the side lets the tongue fall forward and fluids drain, keeping the airway clear. Keep checking breathing until help arrives."),
("A worker on a building site cuts his arm on sheet metal and blood is spurting. What should the guard do first?",
 "Press firmly on the wound", ["Rinse it under the tap", "Give him a painkiller", "Let it bleed to clean it"],
 "Firm direct pressure, with a dressing if one is to hand, controls bleeding. Call 998 for severe bleeding; washing comes later for minor wounds."),
("A choking adult cannot speak or cough. What is the first step?",
 "Up to five back blows", ["Give water to drink", "Lie them on their back", "Sweep the mouth blindly"],
 "Give up to five firm back blows between the shoulder blades, then up to five abdominal thrusts, and repeat. Call 998 if it does not clear."),
("A cleaner splashes boiling water on her hand. How should the burn be cooled?",
 "Under cool running water", ["Ice cubes held on it", "Butter or cooking oil", "Toothpaste spread over it"],
 "Cool running water for 20 minutes reduces damage. Ice, butter and toothpaste can worsen the burn or cause infection."),
("A guard on an outdoor post in August becomes confused, stops sweating and has hot, dry skin. This suggests:",
 "Heatstroke", ["Mild thirst", "A common cold", "Low blood pressure only"],
 "Hot, dry skin and confusion mean the body's cooling has failed. It is a medical emergency: call 998 and cool the person quickly."),
("Which action helps a colleague with heat exhaustion?",
 "Shade and sips of water", ["Keep them working slowly", "Give them a hot drink", "Wrap them in a blanket"],
 "Rest in a cool place, sips of water and loosened clothing help. If they get worse or become confused, call 998."),
("How can a guard on an outdoor gate in summer reduce the risk of heat illness?",
 "Drink often, use shade", ["Drink only at shift end", "Wear extra warm layers", "Skip meals to stay light"],
 "Regular water, shade, breaks and suitable clothing protect against heat illness. Thirst is a late sign of dehydration."),
("A guest has a seizure beside a hotel pool. What should the guard avoid?",
 "Holding the person down", ["Moving chairs out of the way", "Cushioning their head", "Timing how long it lasts"],
 "Restraining a person during a seizure can injure them. Clear the area, protect the head, time it and call 998 if it lasts long or repeats."),
("A guard with diabetes skipped lunch and is now sweating, shaky and confused but can swallow. What should help?",
 "Something sugary to eat", ["A large plate of rice", "His insulin injection", "Only a glass of water"],
 "These are signs of low blood sugar. Fast sugar by mouth works in minutes; insulin would lower it further. Call 998 if he does not improve."),
("A person faints at a hot bus stop near the site. How can the guard help recovery?",
 "Lay them down, legs raised", ["Sit them up straight at once", "Splash them with cold water", "Give them coffee"],
 "Lying flat with the legs raised helps blood return to the brain. Most people recover quickly; keep them cool and watch them."),
("A delivery rider is knocked off a scooter at a car park barrier and says his neck hurts. What should the guard do?",
 "Keep him still, call 998", ["Help him stand up slowly", "Take his helmet off quickly", "Turn his head to check"],
 "Neck pain after a fall may mean spinal injury. Keep the head and neck still and leave the helmet on unless the airway is blocked."),
("A guard must lift a heavy box of radios. What is the safe way to lift?",
 "Bend the knees, load close", ["Bend the back and twist", "Lift fast with straight legs", "Hold it at arm's length"],
 "Bending the knees, keeping the back straight and the load close to the body reduces back injury. Get help for loads that are too heavy."),
("A guard notices oil spilled on a mall floor. What should the guard do?",
 "Cordon it off and report it", ["Walk around it and move on", "Cover it with a newspaper", "Wait for someone to slip"],
 "A wet or oily floor causes falls. Warn people with a sign or cordon and get it cleaned up straight away."),
("A guard directing traffic at a car park entrance should wear:",
 "A high-visibility vest", ["Dark clothing to blend in", "A loose scarf", "Sunglasses at night"],
 "High-visibility clothing helps drivers see the guard, especially at night and in dust. Dark clothes make the guard hard to see."),
("A guard is giving first aid to a bleeding stranger. Why should the guard wear gloves?",
 "To prevent infection", ["To look professional", "To keep hands cool", "To leave no fingerprints"],
 "Gloves protect both the guard and the casualty from blood-borne infection. Hands are washed afterwards."),
("When should a guard learn where the site's first aid kit and AED are kept?",
 "Before any incident", ["Only after an emergency", "When the client asks", "Only on the first day"],
 "Knowing where equipment is saves time. Guards check the locations and that kits are stocked during patrols."),
("A guard finds a person electrocuted and still touching a live cable. What must the guard do first?",
 "Switch off the power first", ["Pull them off by the arm", "Pour water over them", "Start CPR straight away"],
 "Touching the person while the current flows can electrocute the rescuer. Switch off at the source, then give first aid and call 998."),
("After any first aid given on site, the guard should:",
 "Record it in the log", ["Tell no one", "Post it on social media", "Ask the casualty to pay"],
 "Recording first aid shows what was done and when, informs the ambulance crew and meets the site's health and safety rules."),
]
